  unit: { count: 263, covers: "The count is derived from the files listed above, and this note lists EVERY test file the range touches. The previous tip's note named fewer and read 234; the figure here is not 234 plus that many new tests, it is a wider list of the same work, and the two numbers are not comparable. What the suites actually stand at is the figure that follows: 1137 Vitest tests in 55 files and 36 shell tests are green at the tip, beside the core workspace's untouched 754 (313 in trawler, 441 in xtask) - this WU changes no file under src/, xtask/ or tests/, with eslint, tsc and the closed-vocabulary rule clean at 0 live design-allow suppressions. THE FIRST HALF, the twelve P1 findings. TaskStep +7 - the number and title, a purpose line where one is given and none where it is not, a state rendered only where a resolver produced one, the dimmed variant keeping its number, and the slot below the state. The shell +9 - the combined act as one elevation on macOS with the proxy write following the trust write, one UAC prompt on Windows, a host with no automatic path, the two single commands proved unchanged by the combined one, the three wordings macOS and Windows use for a dismissed or unshowable prompt, a real failure that is not one of them, the empty-stderr fallback, the reading that carries the address it classified and the one that has none, and the replacement ask that names the proxy and says it does not come back. The copy as literal assertions against the archived run's copy table - the title and purpose, the question, the six task states of this machine, the un-probeable promise, the primary action and its one-prompt line, the cancelled prompt and its disclosure, the foreign-proxy sentence with and without an address, the network task's single consequence line, the refusal, the arriving remedy and the end - with the seven machine words asserted ABSENT over all eight platforms rather than spot-checked, each task's state traced to the probe, the traffic layer or the reach state it names, the dimming rule off-machine, the Simulator's two stores, the address offer, the probe row's scope, the tick's scope and the single scroller. setup.test.ts over the platform vocabulary; verdicts.test.ts over all seventeen states the three resolvers return, each against StatusLine's ~40-char contract. Plus the six review rounds' regression tests, every one written before its fix. THE SECOND HALF, the owner's reading at the window. CommandBlock +7 - the command verbatim and uncut, its data-wire and mono family, the surface that sets it apart from the prose above it, the icon-form copy that its adjacency licenses, and exactly one control in the block. Select +2 - the trigger fills the component's box so a host can size it, and with no width from the host it is the box it always was. setup.test.ts +30 - the command/value split asserted in BOTH directions over all eight platforms (no value opens with a tool's name, every command does), the three kinds of aside kept out of the numbered sequence, each caveat named where it now lives, the alternatives named where they now live, the surviving step counts pinned, and combine merging two tasks' routes without turning an aside into a step or an empty pair into a throw. Onboarding.test.tsx +14 - the picker's eight options and the answer it carries unopened, the width held across a change of answer, the open-guide rule per platform and once a request has decrypted, a command rendered as a block and a value kept inside the sentence naming its field, the try-it command as a command too, a caveat under the steps and absent from them, a second route behind a closed summary, and the column's class. ChoiceTiles.test.tsx -9, with the entry it tested. And the window round's own review, which refuted its one candidate and named a coverage gap on the way past: the one-guide rule is now an invariant over all eight platforms rather than two - at most one guide open, the open one the first, a guide a button already answers never the open one, and none open once a request has decrypted (+25). THE THIRD HALF, the owner's reading of the window round's own column. Onboarding.test.tsx +1 - the column carries a gap, the task list carries a gap, and no zone the column holds carries a pt- of its own. The rule is asserted and not the pixels it produces: jsdom computes no layout, so a test that read a gap back would be reading the class it had just been handed. The third clause is the one a regression trips - a zone that re-grows its own top padding is a second spacing rule, and nothing reconciles it with the first. Nothing else in the suite moved, which is the claim this round makes: 1133 of the 1134 are the same tests passing unchanged, because a rhythm changes no sentence, no state and no control. AND ROUND 9, the rhythm round's own review, which found a real important in the FIRST half's code that eight earlier rounds over the same bytes had not raised: declining the replace-a-foreign-proxy confirm resolves null, which useAction lands as nothing rather than failed, and neither SETUP_NOTE nor SETPROXY_NOTE had an entry for that state - so ActionNote rendered nothing at all and the button re-enabled with the same label. Onboarding.test.tsx +3, written failing first: the combined action names both halves it did not do, the Simulator's proxy action names the one half it has, and neither reads as a failure - the third pins the state as nothing and keeps the failure wording and the tool-output disclosure away, which is what makes the fix honest rather than merely present." }
  integration: { count: 71, covers: "The seams this WU moves, asserted where the screen sits inside App against the API stub and a mocked invoke - no half observable from a unit test of either side. F27: Set up this Mac fetches the PEM and invokes the one combined command with the split address, and no set_proxy runs beside it; the Simulator's proxy-only action still invokes set_proxy on its own, and its reversal still invokes unset_proxy. F18: a refusal crosses the IPC boundary as a refusal - a rejection is an arbitrary value, so the test asserts the structured failure survives it, the screen words the one the user caused, the tool's text stays under a disclosure, and a real failure keeps the OS's own words and the manual route. F17 in all three of its cases: a foreign proxy is confirmed before it is replaced and a refusal writes nothing, an allowed replacement goes through once, and a machine with no proxy is asked nothing. F22: the list's first paint after the machine's captured event says setup worked, it dismisses, set up another device returns to setup and takes the banner with it, and a launch that finds capture already working announces nothing. Plus the seams the rebuilt screen inherits, re-pointed at what it renders: the save writes the certificate under the name the steps run against, the probes drive both tasks and are re-read by check again and by window focus, a tunnel settles the last task and puts its remedy beside it, and the reach seam's confirm, refusal, arming, disarming and naming are unchanged. The window round re-points every off-machine case through the picker rather than through a tile, which is the one integration-visible change it makes: the platform axis is a popup again, so the suite drives it as one." }
  e2e: { waived: "The epic owes the e2e and W6 writes it, against the screen this WU leaves - the spine rewritten on the platform axis with a coverage map over the 8 assertions the current spec holds. ui/e2e/specs/onboarding.spec.ts is stale at this tip on purpose: its assertions describe the screen W3 built, and re-pointing them here would land a spec asserting a shape one WU of the epic still has to close around. The window round sharpens the reason rather than weakening it: the platform axis changed control twice inside this unit, so an e2e written at either tip would have been rewritten at the other. Recorded as the epic's, per the charter's additive rule." }
